module logging v1.0.0;

// Application logging. Import Logger from logging/logger; destinations live
// in logging/stream and logging/write, and filters in logging/filter.
//
// ```okkama
// import "logging/logger" take Logger;
// async main() is int {
//     let log = Logger("shop");
//     log.info("started");
//     await log.drain();
//     0;
// }
// ```
//
// # Servers and async code
//
// Logger defaults to StderrLogs(): the same text lines as TextStreamLogs(Stderr()),
// formatted on the calling task, then handed to a bounded host buffer. A host
// writer thread writes them to stderr without blocking the task scheduler.
// Use StderrLogs(json: true) for JSON lines; zone: selects the text time zone.
// Its limit: defaults to 1048576 bytes and sets the shared process-wide limit
// once at construction, including the in-flight line. Share one destination
// when several loggers need the same policy; the last construction sets it.
//
// At shutdown, await log.drain() after the tasks that log have settled.
// StderrLogs waits for already accepted lines for at most two seconds. The host
// also drains at exit and before an uncaught failure report. A timed-out drain
// can leave lines pending. WebApplication.listen() drains its logger after the
// server settles, including after SIGTERM or SIGINT.
//
// Overflow drops whole lines. The next line the host writes is preceded by
// "okkama: N log lines dropped"; logs.dropped() reads cumulative process-wide
// losses. EPIPE and other sink failures are silent: the host drops subsequent
// lines and counts them. Records that fail formatting are discarded before
// reaching the host and are not included in dropped().
// Captured test output and the threadless playground receive bytes directly;
// their writes ignore the buffer limit.
//
// Stderr() and TextStreamLogs remain synchronous. QueuedLogs is for genuinely
// asynchronous LogDelivering destinations; wrapping a synchronous stream in
// QueuedLogs does not keep that stream's write from blocking the scheduler.
// Every LogWriting destination has async drain(); synchronous destinations do
// nothing, and wrappers drain their destinations. NamedLogging, child loggers
// and FieldLogging forward drain() to the same shared destination.
